# Clock card

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The Clock card shows the current time on a dashboard, with an optional title and date. In YAML it is `type: clock`. It suits a wall-mounted tablet. For anything where the exact time matters to someone using a screen reader, add the time as text as well.

This page covers:

- Clock card
  - `hui-clock-card`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

Inside one card:

1. An optional title, on one line, centred.
2. The time, as digits or as a clock face.
3. An optional date in words.

The card has no buttons and nothing to select.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-clock-card"><code>hui-clock-card</code></h3>

```yaml
type: clock
title: Hallway
clock_style: digital
clock_size: medium
date_format:
  - weekday-long
  - day-numeric
  - month-long
```

Keys:

- `clock_style` is `digital` (the default) or `analog`.
- `clock_size` is `small` (the default), `medium` or `large`.
- `show_seconds` shows seconds. `seconds_motion` is `continuous` or `tick` on the analogue face.
- `time_format` chooses how the time is written, such as 12-hour or 24-hour.
- `time_zone` is a time zone name such as `Europe/London`.
- `no_background` removes the card background.
- `date_format` is a list of parts, such as `weekday-long`, `day-numeric` and `month-long`, with separators.
- On the analogue face only: `border`, `ticks` (`none`, `quarter`, `hour` or `minute`) and `face_style` (`markers`, `numbers_upright` or `roman`).

The card needs at least 3 columns for a small digital clock, and more for the larger sizes. The analogue face needs 2 rows or more.

Style keys: `--ha-clock-card-digital-font-size-small`, `-medium` and `-large`, and the analogue face keys `--ha-clock-card-analog-face-background`, `-border-radius` and `-padding`. Civica sets none of them. The time, hands and title use `primary-text-color`, which is `#0B0C0C` in the light theme and `#FFFFFF` in the dark theme. The analogue border uses `divider-color`.

Civica gives every card a 1px border with square corners and no shadow through its `card-mod-card-yaml` setting. The clock inherits that.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It is the native card, so it follows Home Assistant’s language and time settings.
- The analogue face has a fixed English label, “Analog clock”, and the label does not give the time. A screen reader does not read the time from it.
- The digital time is drawn as separate pieces of text. It has no live region, so a screen reader does not announce each minute. Nothing announces the time on request either.
- The card has no setting to stop the seconds moving. Leave `show_seconds` off.
- Roman numerals suit few audiences. Avoid `roman` for a household dashboard.

If a person needs the exact time, show the time with a template or an entity state beside the clock.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use the digital style by default. If you use the analogue face, put the time in words somewhere nearby. Pick 12-hour or 24-hour time once and use it everywhere on the dashboard. Show the date with the weekday in words.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: the analogue face needs a text alternative that you add.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text and 1.4.10 Reflow: check the card at 320 CSS pixels.
- 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide: moving seconds are a risk, so keep them off.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: the card does not announce time changes.
- 1.4.3 Contrast: measure the time against the card background in your own theme.

See [Cards](cards.html) for layout and [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html) for the checks.

